WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:05.520
Whereas the ANY key will return a set of&nbsp;
records matching a WHERE clause in a subquery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.880
you may want to know if you have a&nbsp;
subset of data to where every record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.720
matches the same or similar criteria. We&nbsp;
can do this using the ALL keyword. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:21.920
take the query I used with the ANY keyword and&nbsp;
change ANY to ALL. We will only see results if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.440
every product has had an order with a quantity&nbsp;
of at least ten. I will execute this query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.440
and we have no records. I will change&nbsp;
the number in the criteria from ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:43.840
to one. Upon executing this again, still no&nbsp;
records. However, if I change the ALL back to ANY,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:53.440
we see that 266 of our products have sold at&nbsp;
least one time. This next part has nothing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:01:00.480
do with the ANY or ALL keywords. However, we may&nbsp;
want to compare how many products were sold versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.480
how many we have in a real-life situation. I will&nbsp;
select just the portion of the query that deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:15.840
with the Production.Product table and execute it.&nbsp;
We have 504 products. Two hundred sixty-six have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:23.200
sold. One may want to see what is going on&nbsp;
with the rest of the products in real life.

